# A non-strict byte index answers a byte in range and none off the end, so it
# builds a two-field option. When the value goes straight into a dispatch whose
# arms are byte literals and `none`, that option is taken apart again at the
# door — and the emitter used to write the taking-apart as an extractvalue
# pair, an icmp and a select, four instructions on the hot path of every
# decoder loop. The index now merges the two arms as one i64 (the byte, or 256
# for none) and the dispatch reads that.
#
# The two spellings have to agree about every case: a byte in range, the first
# and last positions, and both ways of being off the end. 256 is the sentinel
# the crossing uses, and no byte can be 256, which is what makes it free -- but
# only if the miss edge really carries it. Written against the printed answers
# rather than against the emitted IR, because the IR is the current shape and
# the answers are the contract.
import "std/text"

fn kind cs p
  at_byte cs[p] p

fn at_byte 65 _
  "A"

fn at_byte 66 _
  "B"

fn at_byte none p
  "none at {p}"

fn at_byte c p
  "byte {c} at {p}"

cs = text/bytes "AB0"

pub play =
  print "{kind cs 1}"
  >> print "{kind cs 2}"
  >> print "{kind cs 3}"
  >> print "{kind cs 4}"
  >> print "{kind cs 0}"
